1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:09,258
Welcome to the complete platform walkthrough of Inspironics Capital Cortex, the autonomous capital intelligence and fundraising engine.

2
00:00:09,828 --> 00:00:14,842
Over the next forty-five minutes, we will open every module, look inside each one,

3
00:00:14,862 --> 00:00:21,288
and follow exactly how a funding opportunity travels from first discovery to a human-approved submission.

4
00:00:21,858 --> 00:00:27,460
Everything you will see comes straight from the platform's code, its configuration and its live screens.

5
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:30,662
Here is our route.

6
00:00:30,682 --> 00:00:40,275
We start with the vision and the seven invariants that govern every design decision, then the OCIF architecture, the technology stack and the security model.

7
00:00:40,845 --> 00:00:49,520
Next, we tour the operator cockpit, and then go layer by layer: perception, representation and the knowledge graph, reasoning and scoring,

8
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:52,986
memory and relationships, and strategy and forecasting.

9
00:00:53,556 --> 00:01:02,030
Then the thirteen-agent council, governance and trust, and the actuation layer with proposals, the data room, board reports and alerts.

10
00:01:02,601 --> 00:01:10,210
We finish with administration, the platform core, one complete end-to-end journey, and the evidence of quality behind it all.

11
00:01:10,230 --> 00:01:13,995
Use the chapter markers in your player to jump to any section.

12
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:20,656
Raising capital is a full-time job hidden inside every growing company.

13
00:01:20,676 --> 00:01:29,035
Opportunities are scattered across grant portals, investor networks, inboxes and spreadsheets, and the best ones are easy to miss.

14
00:01:29,605 --> 00:01:40,016
Capital Cortex continuously discovers these opportunities, classifies them, scores them against your organisation's profile, and helps your team pursue the strongest fits.

15
00:01:40,585 --> 00:01:50,751
It covers eleven capital instrument classes: venture equity, private equity, strategic corporate venture, grants, government programs, university programs,

16
00:01:50,771 --> 00:01:52,788
and foundation and ESG funding.

17
00:01:53,358 --> 00:01:58,270
Plus debt facilities, convertibles, equipment finance and revenue-based financing.

18
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:04,655
And one rule sits above everything else: nothing leaves the system without explicit human approval.

19
00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:09,925
The whole platform is anchored on seven invariants.

20
00:02:09,945 --> 00:02:16,688
They are not guidelines in a prompt; wherever possible, they are enforced in code and in the database itself.

21
00:02:17,258 --> 00:02:18,476
I1, no fabrication.

22
00:02:18,496 --> 00:02:21,538
Nothing is asserted without a traceable source.

23
00:02:21,558 --> 00:02:28,510
Every knowledge table has a database check that requires either a source reference or a declared inference.

24
00:02:28,530 --> 00:02:31,507
Missing data is shown as a gap, never guessed.

25
00:02:32,077 --> 00:02:34,140
I2, explainability everywhere.

26
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:41,153
Every recommendation stores its evidence, confidence and reasoning trail at the moment it is created.

27
00:02:41,173 --> 00:02:42,751
I3, governed actuation.

28
00:02:42,771 --> 00:02:49,139
Anything outbound goes through an outbox, with a signed approval bound to the exact content.

29
00:02:49,709 --> 00:02:50,750
I4, zero trust.

30
00:02:50,770 --> 00:02:56,197
Every service re-checks authentication and authorisation at its own boundary.

31
00:02:56,217 --> 00:02:58,036
I5, provenance continuity.

32
00:02:58,056 --> 00:03:03,482
Provenance captured at intake survives unbroken all the way to the dashboard.

33
00:03:04,052 --> 00:03:09,735
I6, the system learns only from realised outcomes, never from its own predictions.

34
00:03:09,755 --> 00:03:13,352
And I7, large language models never compute numbers.

35
00:03:13,372 --> 00:03:21,907
Scores, forecasts, runway and pipeline come from deterministic code; language models may only narrate them, with citations.

36
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:29,375
The architecture is called OCIF, the Octagonal Cognitive Intelligence Framework.

37
00:03:29,395 --> 00:03:34,011
Eight cognitive layers sit around one shared Capital Knowledge Graph.

38
00:03:34,581 --> 00:03:36,933
L1 Perception brings signals in.

39
00:03:36,953 --> 00:03:41,750
L2 Representation classifies them and writes them into the graph.

40
00:03:41,770 --> 00:03:45,604
L3 Memory keeps relationships and context over time.

41
00:03:46,174 --> 00:03:49,505
L4 Reasoning computes the explainable score.

42
00:03:49,525 --> 00:03:55,827
L5 Strategic Intelligence handles the pipeline, runway forecasting and the Copilot.

43
00:03:55,847 --> 00:03:59,026
L6 Agency runs the thirteen-agent council.

44
00:03:59,596 --> 00:04:05,766
L7 Governance and Trust is the gatekeeper: citations, policies, approvals and audit.

45
00:04:05,786 --> 00:04:14,018
And L8 Actuation and Experience delivers the API, the cockpit, proposals, the data room, board packs and alerts.

46
00:04:14,588 --> 00:04:22,764
Crucially, realised outcomes flow back along the L8 to L1 edge, so every won or lost deal makes the next ranking smarter.

47
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:28,255
Technically, Capital Cortex is a modular monolith.

48
00:04:28,275 --> 00:04:36,692
One Python codebase runs both the FastAPI server and the background workers, with module boundaries enforced by import rules,

49
00:04:36,712 --> 00:04:40,404
so any module can be split out later without a rewrite.

50
00:04:40,974 --> 00:04:48,800
Storage is a single Postgres 16 database, extended with Apache AGE for the graph and pgvector for embeddings.

51
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:54,991
Redis Streams carries events between layers, and MinIO stores documents and cold data.

52
00:04:55,561 --> 00:05:04,987
Keycloak provides login with multi-factor authentication, Open Policy Agent decides every authorisation request, and Vault holds the secrets.

53
00:05:05,557 --> 00:05:11,775
OpenTelemetry sends traces, logs and metrics to Tempo, Loki, Prometheus and Grafana.

54
00:05:11,795 --> 00:05:19,795
The cockpit itself is React 18 with TypeScript, Vite and Tailwind, plus ECharts, Cytoscape and FullCalendar.

55
00:05:20,365 --> 00:05:25,194
The whole stack starts with a single make up command, in about two minutes on a warm machine.

56
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:29,993
The repository mirrors the architecture.

57
00:05:30,013 --> 00:05:40,504
Platform core holds the shared, domain-free plumbing: authentication, policy, audit, the event bus, the LLM router, the database helpers and observability.

58
00:05:41,074 --> 00:05:50,635
The cortex package holds one sub-package per OCIF layer, from L1 perception to L8 actuation, plus the worker and the phase registry.

59
00:05:51,205 --> 00:05:53,553
Apps web is the operator cockpit.

60
00:05:53,573 --> 00:06:04,028
Config holds the business rules as data: roles, Rego policies, the taxonomy, retention, adapters, agents, scoring profiles and document templates.

61
00:06:04,598 --> 00:06:13,270
Alongside sit the Alembic migrations, the infrastructure definitions, four test suites, the evaluation gates and the documentation set,

62
00:06:13,290 --> 00:06:18,935
including a traceability matrix that links every requirement to code, screens and tests.

63
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:23,117
Security starts with identity.

64
00:06:23,137 --> 00:06:33,434
Keycloak issues short-lived OpenID Connect tokens, and multi-factor authentication with time-based one-time passwords is required for administrators,

65
00:06:33,454 --> 00:06:35,027
approvers and auditors.

66
00:06:35,597 --> 00:06:36,856
There are six roles.

67
00:06:36,876 --> 00:06:38,327
Admin, for the founder.

68
00:06:38,347 --> 00:06:42,612
Analyst, who works the pipeline, drafts collateral and runs agents.

69
00:06:42,632 --> 00:06:46,065
Approver, the human in the loop for anything outbound.

70
00:06:46,635 --> 00:06:51,154
Auditor, with read-only oversight of every decision and the audit chain.

71
00:06:51,174 --> 00:06:53,927
Executive, for dashboards and board reports.

72
00:06:53,947 --> 00:06:56,701
And Service, for the platform's own workers.

73
00:06:57,271 --> 00:07:04,981
On top of role-based access, attribute-based rules check ownership, document classification and capital class.

74
00:07:05,001 --> 00:07:12,150
Open Policy Agent decides on every request and fails closed, and approvals demand a fresh step-up MFA.

75
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:20,100
External content is always treated as data, never as instructions, which defends the agents against prompt injection.

76
00:07:22,220 --> 00:07:24,025
Let's open the operator cockpit.

77
00:07:24,045 --> 00:07:26,705
This is the app shell that frames every screen.

78
00:07:27,275 --> 00:07:35,337
On the left, navigation is grouped by the cognitive loop: overview, perceive and represent, remember and reason, act, and govern.

79
00:07:35,907 --> 00:07:43,826
Across the top: global search, with a Control K command palette to jump to any opportunity, investor, contact or action.

80
00:07:44,396 --> 00:07:54,488
Then the LLM budget meter, showing today's spend against the daily cap, the approval inbox badge, the alerts bell, the Copilot button and the user and role menu.

81
00:07:55,058 --> 00:07:58,072
The amber ribbon across the top marks demo data.

82
00:07:58,092 --> 00:08:04,014
Every synthetic record is flagged, so it can never be mistaken for real investors or programs.

83
00:08:04,584 --> 00:08:16,991
Every page also carries a badge naming its OCIF layer and requirement, like L5 slash L8, FR-07 here, so you always know which part of the architecture you are looking at.

84
00:08:19,100 --> 00:08:22,249
Now layer by layer, starting with L1, perception.

85
00:08:22,269 --> 00:08:27,812
Its job is to bring capital signals in, continuously, safely and with full provenance.

86
00:08:28,382 --> 00:08:31,695
Each source is an adapter declared in a YAML file.

87
00:08:31,715 --> 00:08:40,027
Adding a source is configuration, not code: a key, a kind, a schedule, a rate limit, a field mapping and a terms-of-use note.

88
00:08:40,597 --> 00:08:46,298
The ingestion worker fetches raw items and normalises each one into a standard Signal.

89
00:08:46,318 --> 00:08:54,215
Provenance is recorded for every item: the source, the URL, the fetch time, the adapter version and the raw object key.

90
00:08:54,785 --> 00:09:00,982
Each signal is de-duplicated by a content hash, so fetching the same content twice inserts nothing.

91
00:09:01,002 --> 00:09:05,127
It is then published to the signals.raw stream for the next layer.

92
00:09:05,697 --> 00:09:07,236
The worker is idempotent.

93
00:09:07,256 --> 00:09:15,468
It retries with exponential backoff and jitter, and poison messages go to a dead-letter queue that operators can inspect and replay.

94
00:09:16,038 --> 00:09:23,382
Adapters honour robots.txt and rate limits, and the platform never scrapes networks whose terms forbid it.

95
00:09:23,402 --> 00:09:27,551
Only official APIs, user-authorised exports or manual entry.

96
00:09:29,660 --> 00:09:33,459
Twelve adapter configurations ship with the platform.

97
00:09:33,479 --> 00:09:43,116
The Grants.gov public API is enabled out of the box, and templates exist for the EU Funding and Tenders portal and UKRI opportunities.

98
00:09:43,686 --> 00:09:53,568
Files and people are covered too: CSV and Excel upload for investor lists, CRM exports and financials, plus manual entry.

99
00:09:53,588 --> 00:09:58,560
Generic RSS and HTML adapters handle feeds and listing pages.

100
00:09:59,130 --> 00:10:08,498
For relationship data, there are read-only, opt-in adapters for an IMAP mailbox, Microsoft Graph mail and calendar, and ICS calendar feeds.

101
00:10:09,068 --> 00:10:19,092
And a data-room folder watcher registers new documents from object storage as unapproved, because approving a document for packages is always a human decision.

102
00:10:21,220 --> 00:10:25,625
This is the Sources and Ingestion screen: the adapter registry and its health.

103
00:10:26,195 --> 00:10:33,546
Each row shows the source, its kind, and the terms-of-use note that explains what the platform is allowed to do with that source.

104
00:10:34,116 --> 00:10:37,830
Health, schedule, last run and items per run come next.

105
00:10:37,850 --> 00:10:47,606
Grants.gov is healthy, runs every six hours, and its last run fetched one hundred twenty-four items: thirty-three new and ninety-one duplicates.

106
00:10:48,176 --> 00:10:52,922
Operators can run any adapter now, enable or disable it, or upload a file.

107
00:10:52,942 --> 00:11:00,392
Errors are counted on the run without stopping it, and a dead-letter queue viewer lets a failed message be replayed.

108
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:03,194
Quick check.

109
00:11:03,214 --> 00:11:06,347
Grants.gov returns the same program twice in one day.

110
00:11:06,367 --> 00:11:08,369
What does the ingestion worker do?

111
00:11:08,389 --> 00:11:11,225
Pause the video if you'd like to think about it.

112
00:11:15,795 --> 00:11:17,654
The answer: it inserts nothing.

113
00:11:17,674 --> 00:11:24,989
The content hash already exists, so the duplicate is counted and skipped, and the first signal's provenance stays intact.

114
00:11:27,100 --> 00:11:29,639
What does the platform actually store?

115
00:11:29,659 --> 00:11:36,577
Every table carries an organisation ID, so the platform is ready for multi-tenancy or federation later.

116
00:11:36,597 --> 00:11:45,804
Core knowledge tables include sources, signals, organisations, investors, funds, grant programs, financial instruments and opportunities.

117
00:11:46,374 --> 00:11:54,222
Each opportunity holds its class, counterparty, amount band, deadline, pipeline stage, score, factors and completeness.

118
00:11:54,242 --> 00:12:04,866
Relationship tables hold contacts, meetings, relationships and milestones; execution tables hold documents, proposals, approvals, the outbox, alerts, financials,

119
00:12:04,886 --> 00:12:06,386
forecasts and outcomes.

120
00:12:06,956 --> 00:12:15,471
The same knowledge lives in the graph, with seventeen node types and edges such as INVESTS IN, KNOWS, INTRODUCED and SUPPORTS.

121
00:12:15,491 --> 00:12:20,821
One edge is mandatory on every derived node: DERIVED FROM, the provenance link.

122
00:12:20,841 --> 00:12:23,328
Embeddings sit beside it in pgvector.

123
00:12:23,898 --> 00:12:31,394
For demonstrations, a synthetic seed creates about three hundred fictional investors, one hundred twenty grant programs,

124
00:12:31,414 --> 00:12:38,597
six hundred opportunities across twenty-five countries, eighteen months of financials and eighty realised outcomes.

125
00:12:38,617 --> 00:12:42,355
Every row is flagged as demo, and one command purges it all.

126
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:48,827
Layer two, representation, turns signals into structured knowledge.

127
00:12:48,847 --> 00:12:55,047
It starts with the capital-class classifier, which is rules first, driven by the taxonomy file.

128
00:12:55,617 --> 00:12:58,261
Each of the eleven classes collects points.

129
00:12:58,281 --> 00:13:03,899
A class hint from the source gives three points, or two if it is only the source's default.

130
00:13:03,919 --> 00:13:09,537
A keyword in the title gives one and a half, and in the description, zero point seven five.

131
00:13:10,107 --> 00:13:15,775
A matching investor type adds two points, and a matching counterparty kind adds one and a half.

132
00:13:16,345 --> 00:13:17,436
Then two ratios.

133
00:13:17,456 --> 00:13:21,676
Share is the top score divided by the top plus the runner-up.

134
00:13:21,696 --> 00:13:25,637
Coverage is the top score divided by four, capped at one.

135
00:13:25,657 --> 00:13:31,405
Confidence equals share times, zero point five plus zero point five times coverage.

136
00:13:31,975 --> 00:13:39,362
Below zero point seven, a small language model may be consulted, but only if one is configured and within budget.

137
00:13:39,382 --> 00:13:48,015
On the labelled set, the rules alone reach ninety-seven point six percent accuracy, and a class set by a human is never overwritten.

138
00:13:50,140 --> 00:13:57,266
Next, the entity resolver decides whether a counterparty is an organisation we already know.

139
00:13:57,286 --> 00:14:02,936
Names are normalised by removing legal suffixes such as Inc, LLC or GmbH.

140
00:14:03,506 --> 00:14:12,013
Candidates are blocked by trigram name similarity, exact domain and compatible country, then scored with Jaro-Winkler similarity.

141
00:14:12,033 --> 00:14:16,904
An exact domain match scores one, and conflicting countries are penalised.

142
00:14:17,474 --> 00:14:21,579
At zero point nine two or above, records merge automatically.

143
00:14:21,599 --> 00:14:29,286
Between zero point eight and zero point nine two, a new record is created and the pair is queued for human review.

144
00:14:29,306 --> 00:14:31,787
Below that, it is a new organisation.

145
00:14:31,807 --> 00:14:34,356
Every merge is reversible and audited.

146
00:14:34,927 --> 00:14:44,906
The typer then applies sector, ESG, SDG and geography tags, and the graph writer creates vertices, edges and relational mirrors in one transaction,

147
00:14:44,926 --> 00:14:49,464
with a DERIVED FROM edge from every node back to its source signal.

148
00:14:50,034 --> 00:14:59,194
Each opportunity is also embedded with a local, deterministic hashing model, so technology similarity works without any external AI service.

149
00:15:01,300 --> 00:15:03,919
Here is the Capital Knowledge Graph explorer.

150
00:15:03,939 --> 00:15:10,489
This demo graph holds two thousand four hundred twenty-two nodes and two thousand five hundred thirty-one edges.

151
00:15:11,059 --> 00:15:17,742
You choose a starting node type and a layout, and filter by node type: organisations, opportunities and signals.

152
00:15:18,312 --> 00:15:22,028
Click a node to inspect its attributes and its provenance chain.

153
00:15:22,048 --> 00:15:28,098
Double-click expands its neighbours, and dashed edges are provenance links back to the original signals.

154
00:15:28,668 --> 00:15:36,513
On the right, the path finder searches for warm-introduction paths of up to four hops, through people we know and people who introduced us.

155
00:15:37,083 --> 00:15:43,264
Below sits the entity merge review queue, with side-by-side comparison and merge or unmerge.

156
00:15:43,284 --> 00:15:48,386
Administrators also get a read-only Cypher console for direct graph queries.

157
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:58,045
Classified opportunities land in the Opportunity Radar: every scored opportunity across the eleven instrument classes.

158
00:15:58,065 --> 00:16:00,546
Seven hundred twenty-four in this demo.

159
00:16:01,116 --> 00:16:08,731
Faceted filters on the left narrow by capital class, score band, pipeline stage and geography, each with live counts.

160
00:16:09,301 --> 00:16:15,810
The table shows score, opportunity and counterparty, class, band, evidence completeness and amount.

161
00:16:15,830 --> 00:16:20,888
Bands carry both colour and a text label, so colour is never the only signal.

162
00:16:21,457 --> 00:16:26,171
Switch to Kanban, and the same opportunities are arranged by pipeline stage.

163
00:16:26,191 --> 00:16:29,970
Dragging a card changes its stage, and the change is audited.

164
00:16:30,540 --> 00:16:39,352
There is also a map view, saved views and a column picker, plus bulk actions to assign, rescore, send to the council, or archive with a reason.

165
00:16:39,922 --> 00:16:42,479
And every screen supports light and dark themes.

166
00:16:44,580 --> 00:16:48,659
Layer four, reasoning, computes the Capital Opportunity Score.

167
00:16:48,679 --> 00:16:50,841
It uses no language model at all.

168
00:16:50,861 --> 00:16:59,370
Every factor is a deterministic or machine-learned plugin that returns a value between zero and one, its evidence and its method.

169
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:09,080
The score is the sum of each factor's weight times its value, divided by the sum of the weights, but only over factors that actually have evidence.

170
00:17:09,100 --> 00:17:11,199
Missing factors are never imputed.

171
00:17:11,769 --> 00:17:16,605
Completeness is the weight of the available factors divided by the total weight.

172
00:17:16,625 --> 00:17:20,916
It tells you how much of the picture is actually supported by evidence.

173
00:17:21,486 --> 00:17:30,147
The default weights: strategic fit zero point one five, probability of success zero point one three, technology alignment,

174
00:17:30,167 --> 00:17:40,964
stage and relationship strength zero point one two each, funding size and timing zero point one zero, geography and ESG relevance zero point zero eight.

175
00:17:41,534 --> 00:17:47,385
Bands: zero point seven or above is high fit, sent to the council and human review.

176
00:17:47,405 --> 00:17:51,204
Zero point four five to zero point seven is watchlist.

177
00:17:51,224 --> 00:17:53,680
Below that, archive, with a reason.

178
00:17:54,250 --> 00:18:02,636
If completeness is under sixty percent, the band is insufficient evidence, and the opportunity cannot be routed above watchlist.

179
00:18:02,656 --> 00:18:07,430
A hard gate, such as an ineligible geography for a grant, forces archive.

180
00:18:09,540 --> 00:18:11,238
Now the individual factors.

181
00:18:11,258 --> 00:18:20,594
Strategic fit counts how many of your strategic priorities appear in the opportunity, divided by the smaller of three and the number of priorities.

182
00:18:21,164 --> 00:18:32,068
Technology alignment uses a local, deterministic embedding called hash TF ten twenty-four: word unigrams and bigrams, hashed into ten twenty-four dimensions,

183
00:18:32,088 --> 00:18:34,016
log weighted and normalised.

184
00:18:34,036 --> 00:18:39,304
The cosine similarity is divided by zero point three five and capped at one.

185
00:18:39,874 --> 00:18:46,870
Geography scores one for an eligible country, zero point six for the same region, and zero otherwise.

186
00:18:46,890 --> 00:18:51,385
For grants and government programs, ineligibility is a hard gate.

187
00:18:51,955 --> 00:19:01,254
Stage uses a distance matrix: the same stage scores one, one step apart zero point six, two steps zero point two, and three or more zero.

188
00:19:03,380 --> 00:19:09,430
Funding size compares your raise target with the opportunity's amount band on a log ten scale.

189
00:19:09,450 --> 00:19:17,051
If the bands overlap, the value is zero point five plus half the overlap divided by the narrower width, capped at one.

190
00:19:17,071 --> 00:19:21,507
Touching bands give zero point five, and one decade apart gives zero.

191
00:19:22,077 --> 00:19:28,005
ESG relevance is the Jaccard similarity of tags: the intersection divided by the union.

192
00:19:28,025 --> 00:19:33,474
Relationship strength is the maximum warmth across contacts at the counterparty.

193
00:19:34,044 --> 00:19:37,143
Timing is a piecewise curve on days to deadline.

194
00:19:37,163 --> 00:19:48,384
A passed deadline scores zero, within a week zero point two, two weeks zero point five, a month zero point eight, the sweet spot of up to one hundred twenty days scores one,

195
00:19:48,404 --> 00:19:52,477
up to a year zero point seven, and beyond that zero point five.

196
00:19:53,047 --> 00:20:00,048
Probability of success comes from a calibrated machine-learning model when one exists, otherwise from a class prior,

197
00:20:00,068 --> 00:20:06,222
such as eight percent for venture equity or thirty-five percent for debt, clearly labelled as a prior.

198
00:20:08,340 --> 00:20:12,335
The machine-learning scorer trains only on realised outcomes.

199
00:20:12,355 --> 00:20:14,178
Won is one and lost is zero.

200
00:20:14,198 --> 00:20:19,510
Withdrawn is excluded, because it says nothing about the counterparty's decision.

201
00:20:20,080 --> 00:20:27,349
Features are the other factor values with explicit missing indicators, the capital class and the log of the amount.

202
00:20:27,369 --> 00:20:36,096
Two candidates, logistic regression and histogram gradient boosting, are calibrated and compared by five-fold cross-validated Brier score.

203
00:20:36,666 --> 00:20:46,398
The winner is tested on a temporal holdout of the newest twenty percent of outcomes, and it is promoted only if it beats the current model on AUC or Brier score.

204
00:20:46,967 --> 00:20:50,355
It needs at least thirty outcomes and retrains weekly.

205
00:20:50,375 --> 00:20:54,456
And a model trained on demo data never scores real opportunities.

206
00:20:56,580 --> 00:20:57,230
Quick check.

207
00:20:57,250 --> 00:21:01,080
An opportunity has evidence for only half of its total factor weight.

208
00:21:01,100 --> 00:21:02,363
Which band does it get?

209
00:21:06,933 --> 00:21:08,273
Insufficient evidence.

210
00:21:08,293 --> 00:21:17,546
With completeness below sixty percent, the platform refuses to rank it as if the missing half did not matter, and it cannot be routed above watchlist.

211
00:21:19,660 --> 00:21:23,658
This is how the score looks to a user, on the opportunity detail screen.

212
00:21:24,228 --> 00:21:32,967
The header shows class, band, demo flag and stage, then the counterparty, amount band and a live deadline countdown: one hundred days here.

213
00:21:33,537 --> 00:21:38,300
On the right, the rank gauge, and the evidence completeness ring at seventy-eight percent.

214
00:21:38,870 --> 00:21:48,211
The action bar lets you change stage, override the class manually, rescore, run the council, log a meeting, record an outcome, or generate a package.

215
00:21:48,781 --> 00:21:58,058
The factor breakdown shows a bar for every factor, with its weight and method: rule, matrix, hash embedding, log band, piecewise, or machine learning.

216
00:21:58,628 --> 00:22:00,902
Click a factor to open its evidence.

217
00:22:00,922 --> 00:22:07,018
Here, probability of success comes from a calibrated gradient-boosting model, with its model ID.

218
00:22:07,588 --> 00:22:13,351
Factors without evidence appear as dashed bars labelled no evidence, rather than a guessed value.

219
00:22:13,371 --> 00:22:16,868
And the radar chart gives the shape of the fit at a glance.

220
00:22:17,438 --> 00:22:27,584
Further down, tabs cover evidence and sources, the graph neighbourhood, relationships and warm-intro paths, agent recommendations with their reasoning trail, proposals,

221
00:22:27,604 --> 00:22:28,855
and the activity log.

222
00:22:30,980 --> 00:22:34,263
Scoring is configurable in the Scoring Studio.

223
00:22:34,283 --> 00:22:44,532
The organisation profile sets sectors, ESG and SDG tags, target geographies, the raise target, and a description used for technology alignment.

224
00:22:45,102 --> 00:22:48,133
Weight sliders for every factor sit on the left.

225
00:22:48,153 --> 00:22:56,842
The three optional factors from the reference design, thesis match, cost and dilution, and strategic value, are available at weight zero.

226
00:22:57,412 --> 00:23:04,793
Move a slider, and the live re-rank preview on the right shows the new order with rank-change arrows, without saving anything.

227
00:23:05,363 --> 00:23:06,744
Profiles are versioned.

228
00:23:06,764 --> 00:23:15,700
An administrator can diff and activate a profile, which triggers a batch rescore, and a backtest panel compares score bands with realised outcomes.

229
00:23:17,820 --> 00:23:20,427
Layer three is memory and relationships.

230
00:23:20,447 --> 00:23:27,059
Its signature metric is warmth: how strong our relationship with a counterparty really is, right now.

231
00:23:27,629 --> 00:23:34,062
Each interaction contributes its weight times e to the power of minus days elapsed over ninety.

232
00:23:34,082 --> 00:23:45,338
A meeting weighs one, an introduction zero point eight, a call zero point seven, an email reply zero point four, a sent email zero point one, and internal notes zero.

233
00:23:45,908 --> 00:23:51,999
The total is normalised with one minus e to the minus S, giving a value between zero and one.

234
00:23:52,019 --> 00:23:57,781
A single meeting today gives about zero point six three, and it fades as the weeks pass.

235
00:23:58,351 --> 00:24:06,751
When warmth changes, that counterparty's opportunities are rescored automatically, because relationship strength is one of the scoring factors.

236
00:24:07,321 --> 00:24:14,037
Memory itself is tiered: hot in Redis for hours, warm in Postgres for months, cold in object storage.

237
00:24:14,057 --> 00:24:23,174
A nightly consolidation job writes a sourced reflection for every counterparty, and commitments become milestones that expire on a timer.

238
00:24:25,300 --> 00:24:31,899
The Relationships screen brings this to life, with tabs for contacts, the follow-up queue and the commitment tracker.

239
00:24:32,469 --> 00:24:42,676
Each contact shows role, organisation, warmth from zero to one hundred, a twelve-week trend sparkline, last touch, interaction count and open milestones.

240
00:24:43,246 --> 00:24:51,133
Here, Lio Umber at Lumara Beacon Foundation has warmth ninety-two after five interactions, with the last touch one day ago.

241
00:24:51,703 --> 00:24:56,011
Log an interaction, log a meeting or add a contact in one click.

242
00:24:56,031 --> 00:25:03,247
Contact emails are masked, and a follow-up email draft goes to the outbox, never directly to the recipient.

243
00:25:05,380 --> 00:25:07,795
Layer five is strategic intelligence.

244
00:25:07,815 --> 00:25:16,810
First, the weighted pipeline: the sum of each opportunity's mid amount times its probability, split by class, stage, geography and month,

245
00:25:16,830 --> 00:25:20,100
and summed per currency without silent conversion.

246
00:25:20,670 --> 00:25:30,830
The probability comes from the calibrated model when available, otherwise from the stage: discovered five percent, qualified ten, engaged twenty, submitted thirty,

247
00:25:30,850 --> 00:25:34,765
diligence forty-five, term sheet seventy, and committed ninety.

248
00:25:35,335 --> 00:25:38,260
The runway forecast is fully deterministic.

249
00:25:38,280 --> 00:25:41,683
Burn is the trailing three-month average net burn.

250
00:25:41,703 --> 00:25:50,105
Each month, cash equals the previous cash, minus burn adjusted by the scenario, minus planned hires, plus expected inflows:

251
00:25:50,125 --> 00:25:53,871
amount times probability times the scenario multiplier.

252
00:25:54,441 --> 00:26:04,306
Runway is the number of months until cash falls below the minimum buffer, and inflows land after a class-specific decision lag, for example six months for grants.

253
00:26:04,876 --> 00:26:13,014
Three presets ship: base, a downside with twenty percent more burn, half the probability and a two-month delay, and an upside.

254
00:26:13,034 --> 00:26:18,777
With no financial data, the engine returns insufficient data, never a placeholder number.

255
00:26:20,900 --> 00:26:24,260
All of this surfaces in the Executive Command Center.

256
00:26:24,280 --> 00:26:28,597
A live indicator shows updates streaming in as sources are ingested.

257
00:26:29,167 --> 00:26:39,476
Seven KPI tiles: cash, monthly net burn as a trailing three-month average, runway, the probability-weighted pipeline, expected inflows over ninety days,

258
00:26:39,496 --> 00:26:42,399
active opportunities and pending approvals.

259
00:26:42,969 --> 00:26:50,588
Below, the runway chart plots base, downside and upside scenarios, each with a data-table fallback for accessibility.

260
00:26:51,158 --> 00:26:55,922
And the risk rail lists open alerts, such as deadlines two and seven days out.

261
00:26:55,942 --> 00:26:58,682
Every tile drills down to its source records.

262
00:27:00,820 --> 00:27:09,592
In Runway and Forecast, the financial snapshots table shows each month's cash, revenue, opex and net burn, with the source of every row.

263
00:27:10,162 --> 00:27:15,525
Scenario cards compare runway for base, downside, upside and a custom scenario.

264
00:27:16,095 --> 00:27:25,784
The scenario builder adjusts burn change, the inflow probability multiplier, inflow delay, a raise with amount, month and probability, and a hiring plan.

265
00:27:26,354 --> 00:27:33,125
You can even override the probability of individual expected inflows, and the cash curve redraws instantly.

266
00:27:33,145 --> 00:27:36,425
Nothing is saved to the server unless you choose to.

267
00:27:38,540 --> 00:27:42,008
The Capital Copilot is a drawer available on every page.

268
00:27:42,028 --> 00:27:45,434
Here we ask: what is our probability-weighted pipeline?

269
00:27:46,004 --> 00:27:53,015
It retrieves with full-text and vector search, routes the question to deterministic tools, and answers with facts.

270
00:27:53,035 --> 00:27:58,442
Each fact carries citation chips pointing to the tool run and the configuration it used.

271
00:27:59,012 --> 00:28:04,761
Suggested prompts adapt to context, like: what happens to runway if the top grant slips three months?

272
00:28:05,331 --> 00:28:07,793
Every claim passes the citation checker.

273
00:28:07,813 --> 00:28:14,431
If nothing survives, the Copilot says it does not have sourced evidence, and lists exactly what is missing.

274
00:28:16,540 --> 00:28:22,536
The Grant Calendar puts every deadline, submission milestone, follow-up and commitment on one calendar.

275
00:28:23,106 --> 00:28:29,908
Colour shows the capital class; icons and labels show the event type, and overdue items are flagged in red.

276
00:28:30,478 --> 00:28:36,757
Month, week and agenda views are available, and the whole calendar exports to iCal for internal users.

277
00:28:38,860 --> 00:28:50,784
Layer six is agency: a council of thirteen specialist agents, each declared in a YAML file with its role, goal, tools, model tier, triggers, token budget and output schema.

278
00:28:51,354 --> 00:28:53,415
Discovery triages new signals.

279
00:28:53,435 --> 00:28:56,189
Grant handles eligibility and packaging.

280
00:28:56,209 --> 00:28:58,686
Venture Capital matches fund theses.

281
00:28:58,706 --> 00:29:01,183
Private Equity covers growth rounds.

282
00:29:01,203 --> 00:29:06,038
Debt covers facilities, equipment finance and revenue-based financing.

283
00:29:06,058 --> 00:29:08,465
Convertible models SAFEs and notes.

284
00:29:09,035 --> 00:29:14,772
Government Programs and University Programs cover public schemes and research partnerships.

285
00:29:14,792 --> 00:29:18,251
Relationship tracks warmth, intro paths and follow-ups.

286
00:29:18,821 --> 00:29:20,586
Proposal drafts collateral.

287
00:29:20,606 --> 00:29:24,221
Due Diligence maps the data room against the checklist.

288
00:29:24,241 --> 00:29:28,386
Forecasting narrates runway using only forecast engine results.

289
00:29:28,406 --> 00:29:32,220
And Board Intelligence produces the board-ready synthesis.

290
00:29:34,340 --> 00:29:37,090
A LangGraph orchestrator runs each council.

291
00:29:37,110 --> 00:29:42,757
It plans which agents match the opportunity's class, stage and band, then fans them out.

292
00:29:43,327 --> 00:29:50,935
The concurrency governor allows at most four agents in parallel, with a per-task token ceiling and a daily dollar budget.

293
00:29:50,955 --> 00:29:57,113
When a limit is hit, it degrades gracefully and returns partial results clearly marked incomplete.

294
00:29:57,683 --> 00:30:02,067
Positions are collected and converged with a confidence-weighted tally.

295
00:30:02,087 --> 00:30:09,262
Then every claim goes through the citation checker and policy evaluation before anything reaches the approval queue.

296
00:30:09,832 --> 00:30:14,540
Agent tools are deterministic and read-only, or internal-write at most.

297
00:30:14,560 --> 00:30:22,599
Confidence is computed, never generated: the share of claims that passed the citation check, times evidence completeness.

298
00:30:22,619 --> 00:30:26,196
External actions can only ever create an outbox draft.

299
00:30:26,766 --> 00:30:32,348
And every call writes an agent run record, with tokens, cost, model tier and trace ID.

300
00:30:34,460 --> 00:30:43,714
In the Agent Council screen, the roster shows all thirteen agents, with status, tier, runs today, cost, success rate, budget and their declared tools.

301
00:30:44,284 --> 00:30:54,586
This environment has no language model key configured, so the banner states clearly that agents run in deterministic mode: every claim comes straight from a tool result.

302
00:30:55,156 --> 00:30:59,539
Switch to Deliberation, choose an opportunity, and run the council.

303
00:30:59,559 --> 00:31:07,817
Here, five agents responded for an equipment finance opportunity: Debt, Due Diligence, Forecasting, Proposal and Relationship.

304
00:31:08,387 --> 00:31:17,593
Each agent column streams its position live: the tools it called, the facts and gaps it found, a computed confidence, and the citation-check result.

305
00:31:18,163 --> 00:31:21,095
Where evidence is missing, the agent says so.

306
00:31:21,115 --> 00:31:27,391
The Due Diligence agent flags that the data room is empty, as an explicit evidence-required gap.

307
00:31:29,500 --> 00:31:34,459
Layer seven, governance and trust, is where the platform earns its credibility.

308
00:31:34,479 --> 00:31:41,013
Its first gate is the citation checker, applied to every claim from an agent, the Copilot or a proposal.

309
00:31:41,583 --> 00:31:46,929
Rule one: every claim must cite something, and inferences must declare their basis.

310
00:31:46,949 --> 00:31:53,329
Rule two: every reference must resolve to a real record that the requesting user is allowed to see.

311
00:31:53,899 --> 00:32:02,056
Rule three: every number in the text must match a number in the cited records within half a percent, and dates must match exactly.

312
00:32:02,076 --> 00:32:11,553
Rule four: any organisation-like name, such as something Fund or something Capital, must appear in a cited record, so no investor can ever be invented.

313
00:32:12,123 --> 00:32:14,724
Failures go back for at most two revisions.

314
00:32:14,744 --> 00:32:22,341
Anything still failing is stripped and surfaced as a gap, and rejections are metered, so a spike raises a data-quality alert.

315
00:32:24,460 --> 00:32:26,107
Now governed actuation.

316
00:32:26,127 --> 00:32:34,225
When something needs to leave the system, an approval request is bound to the SHA-256 hash of its exact content.

317
00:32:34,795 --> 00:32:42,486
Every decision requires step-up multi-factor authentication, and by default the requester cannot approve their own request.

318
00:32:43,056 --> 00:32:46,531
Rego policies decide when approvals are sufficient.

319
00:32:46,551 --> 00:32:49,067
Outbound items need a human approval.

320
00:32:49,087 --> 00:32:51,602
Grant submissions need two approvers.

321
00:32:51,622 --> 00:32:58,044
Anything with financial terms, like a valuation, a cap or covenants, needs an admin and legal.

322
00:32:58,064 --> 00:33:00,648
And exporting personal data is denied.

323
00:33:01,218 --> 00:33:04,291
Only then is a signed, single-use token issued.

324
00:33:04,311 --> 00:33:11,267
If anyone edits the content afterwards, the hash changes, and a database trigger invalidates the approval.

325
00:33:11,837 --> 00:33:15,553
The outbox sender is the only path to the outside world.

326
00:33:15,573 --> 00:33:26,896
It verifies the token's signature, expiry and single use, re-hashes the payload, re-evaluates policy, and only then delivers, auditing everything in the same transaction.

327
00:33:29,020 --> 00:33:39,024
In the Approval Inbox, pending items are sorted by soonest deadline, with content flags like financial terms or personal data, and the citation result.

328
00:33:39,594 --> 00:33:45,116
The detail pane shows a rendered preview of the exact content: here, an outbound email.

329
00:33:45,686 --> 00:33:54,151
Below it, the changes since the last approved version, and the policy evaluation, which states exactly which requirement is still outstanding.

330
00:33:54,721 --> 00:34:00,348
The Outbox tab shows the outbound pipeline: draft, pending, approved, sent and blocked.

331
00:34:00,368 --> 00:34:04,308
An approved item can be sent; editing it starts a new review.

332
00:34:06,420 --> 00:34:07,135
Quick check.

333
00:34:07,155 --> 00:34:11,480
An approver signs off an email, and then an analyst fixes a typo in it.

334
00:34:11,500 --> 00:34:12,276
What happens?

335
00:34:16,846 --> 00:34:18,604
The approval is invalidated.

336
00:34:18,624 --> 00:34:27,936
The content hash no longer matches, so the database trigger clears the approval, and the outbox will refuse to send until a new review is complete.

337
00:34:30,060 --> 00:34:33,868
Before any collateral leaves, a compliance check scans it.

338
00:34:33,888 --> 00:34:36,773
Guarantees or risk-free claims are blockers.

339
00:34:36,793 --> 00:34:40,073
Forward-looking statements require the disclaimer.

340
00:34:40,093 --> 00:34:47,928
Superlatives need cited support, return projections need legal review, and personal contact data needs a consent basis.

341
00:34:48,498 --> 00:34:52,807
Every important action is written to a hash-chained audit log.

342
00:34:52,827 --> 00:35:03,560
Each entry's hash is the SHA-256 of the previous hash plus the canonical JSON of the entry, and a database trigger blocks updates, deletes and truncation.

343
00:35:04,130 --> 00:35:15,365
Retention is configuration: the audit log is kept seven years and is immutable; signals move to cold storage after two years; hot memory lasts twenty-four hours;

344
00:35:15,385 --> 00:35:17,252
drafts expire after a year.

345
00:35:17,272 --> 00:35:20,188
And legal hold always wins over retention.

346
00:35:22,300 --> 00:35:26,201
The Audit and Compliance screen puts all of this in the auditor's hands.

347
00:35:26,771 --> 00:35:32,064
Press verify chain, and the platform recomputes every hash across the whole log.

348
00:35:32,084 --> 00:35:35,783
Result: chain intact, four hundred seventy-five records.

349
00:35:36,353 --> 00:35:46,889
A compliance report for any period exports to Excel, covering chain status, approvals, external releases, legal holds, retention runs and admin changes.

350
00:35:46,909 --> 00:35:48,903
The export itself is audited.

351
00:35:49,473 --> 00:35:57,687
And retention policies can be dry-run or run now, with each policy's retention period, tier, action and immutability on display.

352
00:35:59,820 --> 00:36:04,728
Layer eight, actuation and experience, is where the work turns into deliverables.

353
00:36:04,748 --> 00:36:09,596
The Proposal Factory builds evidence-backed collateral from the knowledge graph.

354
00:36:10,166 --> 00:36:17,137
There are four package types: a VC pitch, a grant application, a debt facility and an ESG impact memo.

355
00:36:17,157 --> 00:36:20,700
Each package defines its sections and its artefacts.

356
00:36:21,270 --> 00:36:31,016
Eight artefacts in all: pitch deck, executive summary, investment memo, grant narrative, budget, a financial model with live Excel formulas,

357
00:36:31,036 --> 00:36:36,596
technical annex and due-diligence checklist, in Word, PowerPoint, Excel and PDF.

358
00:36:37,166 --> 00:36:43,010
Every claim carries a numbered citation, and every artefact ends with an evidence appendix.

359
00:36:43,030 --> 00:36:52,162
Anything unsupported is rendered as evidence required, which blocks approval until it is resolved, or waived by an admin with an audit record.

360
00:36:54,300 --> 00:37:02,040
The proposal list shows each package with its type, status, version, open gaps, compliance result and generation mode.

361
00:37:02,060 --> 00:37:05,788
The drafts with twelve open gaps cannot be submitted yet.

362
00:37:06,358 --> 00:37:13,644
Open a package, and the editor shows status, version, compliance and the content hash, with submit for approval and send.

363
00:37:14,214 --> 00:37:17,180
The section outline on the left tracks completion.

364
00:37:17,200 --> 00:37:21,242
The centre shows each claim marked as fact, with its citation chips.

365
00:37:21,812 --> 00:37:27,824
And the evidence panel lists every reference cited in the section, alongside any compliance findings.

366
00:37:27,844 --> 00:37:32,065
Versions and exports have their own tabs, with a diff between versions.

367
00:37:34,180 --> 00:37:42,338
The Data Room stores versioned documents in object storage, with folders, drag-and-drop upload, DD tags and classification.

368
00:37:42,908 --> 00:37:54,637
The due-diligence checklist maps documents to ten standard items by their tags: incorporation, cap table, financials, financial model, pitch deck, IP, contracts, team,

369
00:37:54,657 --> 00:37:55,973
ESG and compliance.

370
00:37:56,543 --> 00:38:01,897
An item counts as covered only when a mapped document is in the approved repository.

371
00:38:01,917 --> 00:38:06,760
Here, two of ten are covered, and the rest are flagged as evidence required.

372
00:38:07,330 --> 00:38:14,084
Assemble package builds a package from approved documents only, with a checksum manifest and an access log.

373
00:38:14,104 --> 00:38:18,009
Share links expire, and creating one itself requires approval.

374
00:38:20,140 --> 00:38:28,950
Board Reports generates a one-click board pack for any period, with runway, pipeline, weighted funding, key opportunities, risks and asks.

375
00:38:29,520 --> 00:38:32,894
It is built deterministically from the period's records.

376
00:38:32,914 --> 00:38:38,832
Every statement is cited, and anything without evidence is listed as a gap, rather than filled in.

377
00:38:39,402 --> 00:38:43,127
Each pack shows its compliance result and distribution status.

378
00:38:43,147 --> 00:38:47,898
Distribution waits for approval, like everything else that leaves the platform.

379
00:38:50,020 --> 00:38:53,982
The Alerts Center is driven by a deterministic rule engine.

380
00:38:54,002 --> 00:38:59,989
Here, sixty-seven open alerts: six critical, fifty-seven warnings and four informational.

381
00:39:00,559 --> 00:39:10,462
Five default rules: a new high-score opportunity within twenty-four hours, deadline countdowns at thirty, fourteen, seven and two days, overdue follow-ups,

382
00:39:10,482 --> 00:39:15,072
runway below nine months, and commitments expiring within fourteen days.

383
00:39:15,642 --> 00:39:19,797
Each alert can be acknowledged, snoozed, assigned or resolved.

384
00:39:19,817 --> 00:39:26,193
Alerts go in-app, to internal email or to a webhook, and never directly to external recipients.

385
00:39:28,300 --> 00:39:30,598
Administration lives in one place.

386
00:39:30,618 --> 00:39:35,507
Users and roles are synced from Keycloak, with MFA status for each user.

387
00:39:36,077 --> 00:39:47,748
Tabs cover OPA policies, which can be viewed and tested with sample input, the LLM router tiers and providers, budgets and cost, the taxonomy editor and extension flags.

388
00:39:48,318 --> 00:39:58,101
Budgets and cost shows today's spend against the daily cap, here twenty-five dollars, with per-feature caps for classification, compliance, the Copilot,

389
00:39:58,121 --> 00:40:02,873
council deliberation and convergence, extraction, narration and proposals.

390
00:40:03,443 --> 00:40:08,886
When a cap is reached, agents fall back to deterministic mode instead of spending more.

391
00:40:08,906 --> 00:40:11,021
And every admin change is audited.

392
00:40:13,140 --> 00:40:18,617
Underneath every layer sits platform core: shared foundations, free of domain logic.

393
00:40:19,187 --> 00:40:27,620
The LLM router is vendor neutral, with small, mid and large tiers mapped to Anthropic, Azure OpenAI or self-hosted vLLM.

394
00:40:27,640 --> 00:40:35,439
Every call checks a content-hash cache, pre-checks the budget, calls the provider and records the actual spend.

395
00:40:36,009 --> 00:40:48,455
Cost discipline is built in: scoring uses no LLM at all; extraction and classification use the small tier; deliberation small to mid; and only convergence uses the large tier.

396
00:40:49,025 --> 00:40:59,911
The event bus uses Redis Streams with consumer groups, retries with backoff and jitter, a dead-letter queue, idempotency markers, and a signed actor token on every envelope.

397
00:41:00,481 --> 00:41:10,231
Observability ties it together: OpenTelemetry traces across the API, bus, workers and agents, with SLO alerts for latency, queue depth,

398
00:41:10,251 --> 00:41:14,357
budget breaches and spikes in unsourced-claim rejections.

399
00:41:16,460 --> 00:41:20,479
Let's put it all together, and follow one opportunity from start to finish.

400
00:41:21,049 --> 00:41:23,582
A Grants.gov run fetches a new program.

401
00:41:23,602 --> 00:41:29,995
It is normalised into a signal, tagged with provenance, de-duplicated and published to the stream.

402
00:41:30,565 --> 00:41:36,608
The worker classifies it as a grant, resolves the agency, writes it into the graph and scores it.

403
00:41:36,628 --> 00:41:41,233
It lands in the high band, the Radar updates live, and an alert is raised.

404
00:41:41,803 --> 00:41:43,132
The council convenes.

405
00:41:43,152 --> 00:41:52,829
The Grant, Relationship and Forecasting agents respond, every claim is checked against its citations, and a recommendation lands in the approval queue.

406
00:41:53,399 --> 00:41:55,991
An analyst generates the grant package.

407
00:41:56,011 --> 00:42:04,296
Evidence gaps are resolved, compliance is clean, and two approvers sign off with step-up MFA, as policy requires for grants.

408
00:42:04,866 --> 00:42:09,537
The outbox verifies the token and the hash, and releases the submission.

409
00:42:09,557 --> 00:42:15,465
Months later, the outcome is recorded as won, and that realised outcome retrains the model.

410
00:42:15,485 --> 00:42:16,703
The loop is closed.

411
00:42:18,820 --> 00:42:20,352
How do we know it works?

412
00:42:20,372 --> 00:42:23,133
Quality is enforced by a pipeline of gates.

413
00:42:23,153 --> 00:42:33,220
The closing run passed one hundred eighty-nine unit tests, twenty-one integration tests on the real database image, twenty-four of twenty-four policy tests,

414
00:42:33,240 --> 00:42:35,484
and seven end-to-end browser tests.

415
00:42:36,054 --> 00:42:46,638
Contract testing generated over two thousand one hundred API cases from the OpenAPI spec, and six evaluation gates guard no fabrication, grounding,

416
00:42:46,658 --> 00:42:49,740
classification accuracy and scoring sanity.

417
00:42:50,310 --> 00:42:55,865
A load test on a one-million-node knowledge graph passed at team traffic with zero errors:

418
00:42:55,885 --> 00:43:04,290
dashboard ninety-fifth percentile one point eight seven seconds against a three-second target, the list at one point zero eight seconds,

419
00:43:04,310 --> 00:43:12,963
graph neighbourhood one hundred forty-three milliseconds, paths ninety-one milliseconds, and rescoring one hundred forty-eight milliseconds.

420
00:43:13,533 --> 00:43:22,811
Security scans with Trivy and OWASP ZAP closed every critical and high finding, an authorisation matrix tests every role against every endpoint,

421
00:43:22,831 --> 00:43:29,268
and the backup drill achieved a recovery point under fifteen minutes and a recovery time of seconds.

422
00:43:31,380 --> 00:43:37,292
The platform was delivered in four phases, each ending in a working demo and a green pipeline.

423
00:43:37,862 --> 00:43:46,864
Phase zero, platform core: the full stack, authentication with MFA, the audit chain, the bus, the LLM router, the schema and the React shell.

424
00:43:47,434 --> 00:43:55,265
Phase one: discovery, the knowledge graph and explainable scoring, with live Grants.gov data and real-time updates.

425
00:43:55,285 --> 00:44:00,385
Phase two: memory, relationships, the agent council and governed actuation.

426
00:44:00,955 --> 00:44:12,000
Phase three: the Proposal Factory, data room, board reports, Copilot, compliance, retention and administration, plus the hardening work on load, security and recovery.

427
00:44:12,570 --> 00:44:24,208
Six extension points are already defined as interfaces behind feature flags: a negotiation assistant, a portfolio optimiser, a scenario planner, a finance digital twin,

428
00:44:24,228 --> 00:44:30,940
an ecosystem mapper, and Cortex federation for exchanging events with other Inspironics cortexes.

429
00:44:33,060 --> 00:44:33,614
To recap.

430
00:44:33,634 --> 00:44:43,364
Capital Cortex discovers opportunities from many sources, classifies and resolves them into a knowledge graph, and scores them with transparent formulas.

431
00:44:43,934 --> 00:44:53,329
A council of agents deliberates with cited evidence, every outbound action goes through signed human approval, and realised outcomes retrain the model.

432
00:44:53,899 --> 00:44:55,470
Every number is computed.

433
00:44:55,490 --> 00:44:56,807
Every claim is cited.

434
00:44:56,827 --> 00:44:59,098
And nothing leaves without approval.

435
00:44:59,118 --> 00:45:02,280
Thank you for watching Inspironics Capital Cortex.

